RETAIN_NOTES={
 9:'That\'s Rocco Alemkel. is a complete natural identification answer. Rocco matches the project\'s existing ロッコ portrait caption and item-name spelling; Alemkel is the existing project rendering of アレムケル and a consistent phonetic rendering. Retain this game spelling without assigning a real historical identity or inventing title, kinship or biography. This does not approve every external or catalog use of the name.',
 23:'Sorry! Coming! is idiomatic quick speech from Camille in response to Lil\'s impatience. It preserves apology and immediate willingness to follow without mechanically repeating I am going now.',
 27:'Right behind you, fatty! naturally combines Samwell\'s casual promise to follow with the explicit おでぶ provocation. Retain the source body-size tease at its existing intensity. It is his taunt, not a narrator judgment, a new established nickname or a stronger threat.',
 34:'Bang! Crash! Thud! Clunk! preserves four discrete impact/clatter beats in the FE sound-effect object. These idiomatic English effects carry the comic commotion without naming a broken object, weapon, injury or speaker.',
 37:'FI! Stop throwing things! Ow! is natural alarmed speech and preserves the original runtime FI name, complaint and pain beat. Keep the macro rather than hardcoding Lil. The line does not identify a specific thrown object or certify an injury.',
 40:'Wrong way! Over here! is natural taunting speech after Camille complains about the thrown objects. It preserves Samwell\'s point that the thrower is missing him and he is elsewhere. A literal Where are you throwing? is unnecessary; no successful hit or new location is implied.',
 47:'Sigh... Always left to clean up. is a natural weary private-sounding complaint in this spoken context. The fragment preserves Camille\'s recurring grievance and transition into tidying without a stiff full-sentence expansion or invented employment/household relationship.',
 50:'Hm? What\'s this? naturally conveys the sudden discovery and deictic question. It withholds the object identity until the next line, preserving the source progression.',
 58:'The effective runtime line Camille! What are you doing? Come on, let\'s go! is already natural, uses approved Camille, restores the question and preserves the inclusive summons. The registered name-integration catalog row305 supplies it despite the older stored Kamil line. Retain this effective wording and remove the premature authored break through native formatting. Credit the existing name correction; do not misreport a live Kamil defect or shorten an already good line.',
 62:'Where\'s Samwell? is a complete natural whereabouts question after Lil returns from chasing him. Its abrupt subject shift carries Camille\'s mild surprise in context; adding a spelled-out Huh would be optional rather than a fidelity correction.',
 66:'Who cares about him? is idiomatic irritated dismissal in this exchange. It conveys Lil\'s contemptuous あんなヤツ、知らない without claiming she has never known Samwell or certifying a breakup. Do not force a literal I do not know that man into this familiar ongoing relationship.',
 70:'(Got away again. Samwell\'s quick on his feet.) is natural interior speech. The fragment and agile description preserve Camille\'s inference that Samwell escaped Lil again. Parentheses keep it private; there is no need to insert her name or make Lil overhear a thought.',
 73:'What?! is the natural sharp challenge to Camille\'s expression. It remains compatible with the preceding private thought without claiming Lil heard it.',
 77:'N-nothing. preserves Camille\'s nervous stammer and denial naturally. Its contrast with his composed portrait explanation is part of his voice in this exchange.',
 81:'Hmph! Stop grinning! is natural annoyed English and preserves Lil\'s explicit objection to his visible expression. No explanation of the grin or new romantic implication is needed.',
 84:'S-sorry. Oh, here. preserves Camille\'s stammered apology and deictic redirection to the found object. The brief introduction is natural speech; it does not say he bought it, chose a gift or intended to give it to her. The next question and two denials establish the misunderstanding.',
 88:'What\'s this? A gift? is natural curious speech and keeps Lil\'s proposed gift interpretation as a question. Gift and present are ordinary synonyms here; changing the word would not improve meaning or characterization.',
 91:'N-no, that\'s not... is a natural stammered, unfinished correction to the gift assumption. Preserve its incompleteness and separate occurrence before the second correction; it is not a rejection of Lil herself.',
 103:'Thanks! naturally conveys Lil\'s warm turn from irritation to appreciation while she still assumes the object is a gift. No added declaration, promise or romance is needed.',
 115:'(Was that okay?) naturally expresses Camille\'s private uncertainty about letting the misunderstanding stand. The past-tense reference to his just-completed acquiescence preserves いいのかな in this context. It does not resolve the book\'s ownership or give the narrator\'s approval; retain the question and parentheses.',
}
